{
  "abstract": "Krenn, Gu and Zeilinger (Physical Review Letters 119, 240403, 2017) showed that an experiment built from probabilistic photon-pair sources and linear optics corresponds to an edge-weighted graph: vertices are output paths, edges are pair sources, the colour at each end of an edge is the mode of the photon sent to that path, and each perfect matching is one way for every detector to click. A matching gives every vertex the colour of its edge at that vertex, the \"inherited vertex colouring\" of Krenn's problem statement, and the amplitude of a vertex colouring is the sum, over the perfect matchings inheriting it, of the product of their complex edge weights. Gu, Erhard, Zeilinger and Krenn (PNAS 116, 4147, 2019) develop this weighted interference picture. The experiment produces a D-dimensional GHZ state on N photons exactly when each of the D constant colourings has amplitude 1 and every other colouring has amplitude 0. Krenn, Gu and Soltész ask whether such a graph exists (arXiv:1902.06023, Question 1); the conjecture, known as the Krenn–Gu conjecture, is that it does not once N ≥ 6 and D ≥ 3. For N = 4 a solution with D = 3 does exist, given by the three perfect matchings of K_4, which is why the conjecture starts at six vertices. This formalisation proves the conjecture in full: for every even N ≥ 6 and every D ≥ 3, no assignment of complex weights to the endpoint-coloured edges of K_N satisfies these equations. This is the normalised condition of Chandran, Gajjala and Illickan (MFCS 2024, Sections 1.1 and 1.4), who proved the conjecture when the underlying graph has vertex connectivity at most two or maximum degree at most three; Cervera-Lierta, Krenn and Aspuru-Guzik (Quantum 6, 836, 2022) had excluded the cases (N, D) = (6, 3) and (8, 4) for edges with equal endpoint colours. Here the weights are arbitrary complex numbers and the two ends of an edge may carry different colours; no sparsity, positivity or symmetry is assumed, and a weight on K_N stands for the total weight of any parallel edges with the same endpoint colours. Physically, photon-pair sources and linear optics without further resources cannot produce a GHZ state of local dimension three or more on six or more photons. The Lean statement `MonochromaticQuantumGraph.eqSystem_no_solution_ge6_ge3` and its definitions are those of the Formal Conjectures file `FormalConjectures/Paper/MonochromaticQuantumGraph.lean`. The proof shows that the equations force every edge with nonzero weight to have equal endpoint colours and each colour class to be a single perfect matching, and then applies Bogdanov's obstruction for three such matchings. It uses only the axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.",
